# Chapter 6 · World-System Theory and Gender

> A 2019 MCQ asked who developed world-system theory (**Immanuel Wallerstein**). In 2025 the descriptive paper asked candidates to "critically evaluate the concept of '**gendered globalisation**', focusing on **World-System Theory** and how the globalisation of capitalism has created new forms of patriarchal power that increase gender inequalities."

## 6.1  What world-system theory says

::: thinker Immanuel Wallerstein | *The Modern World-System*, vol. 1 (1974)
Wallerstein argued that since the **sixteenth century** there has been a single **capitalist world-economy**, divided into three zones:
- the **core** — rich, industrial, high-wage countries that control finance and technology;
- the **periphery** — poor countries supplying raw materials and cheap labour;
- the **semi-periphery** — countries in between, which both exploit the periphery and are exploited by the core.
Surplus flows from periphery to core through **unequal exchange**. Countries can move between zones, but the **system** stays unequal.
:::

::: fig pyramid | Wallerstein's world-system.
Core: rich industrial states — finance, technology, high wages
Semi-periphery: middle states — both exploiters and exploited
Periphery: poor states — raw materials, cheap and female labour
:::

How it differs from dependency theory:

1. It studies **one world system**, not pairs of countries.
2. It adds the **semi-periphery**, explaining how some countries (such as South Korea or China) rise.
3. It takes a **long historical view**, from the 1500s.

## 6.2  Adding gender: what the original theory missed

Like dependency theory, early world-system theory was largely **gender-blind**. Feminist scholars extended it in three ways.

### (1) The household as the base of the system

Wallerstein himself, with **Joan Smith** (*Creating and Transforming Households*, ==1992==), argued that capitalism relies on **households** that combine wages with **unpaid work, subsistence farming and petty trade**. Because households survive on more than wages, employers can pay **less than a living wage**. The unpaid labour that fills the gap is mostly **women's**.

### (2) Housewifisation on a world scale

**Maria Mies** (*Patriarchy and Accumulation on a World Scale*, ==1986==) argued that the world-system rests on three linked forms of exploitation: of **nature**, of **colonies** and of **women**. Women everywhere are defined as "housewives", so their work — at home or in export production — can be paid little or nothing.

### (3) The global assembly line

From the 1970s, companies moved manufacturing to **export processing zones** in the periphery and semi-periphery, where they hired mainly **young women** — in electronics, garments and toys. **Annette Fuentes and Barbara Ehrenreich** (*Women in the Global Factory*, ==1983==) called this the **global assembly line**. **Kathryn Ward** (*Women Workers and Global Restructuring*, ==1990==) brought such research into world-system analysis.

## 6.3  "Gendered globalisation" (2025)

::: define Gendered globalisation | the 2025 concept
The idea that globalisation — the integration of economies through trade, investment, finance and migration — is **not gender-neutral**: it relies on, reshapes and often **deepens** gender inequalities, while creating some new opportunities for women.
:::

### New forms of patriarchal power in the globalised world-system

1. **Corporate patriarchy.** Global brands and factory managers control women workers through low wages, long hours, surveillance and harassment — a **workplace patriarchy** added to the family's.
2. **Feminisation of labour.** Jobs once held by men become "women's jobs" as they are made **flexible, informal and low-paid** (**Guy Standing**, "Global Feminization through Flexible Labor", ==1989==).
3. **Global care chains.** Women from the periphery migrate to care for families in the core, leaving their own children with relatives — a transfer of care from poor to rich (**Arlie Hochschild**, ==2000==).
4. **Debt and austerity.** Core-controlled lenders impose spending cuts that shift the burden of care onto women (Chapter 12).
5. **Commodification of bodies.** Growth of global sex industries, **trafficking**, commercial surrogacy and beauty industries.
6. **Cultural backlash.** Rapid change can provoke local patriarchal reactions — tighter control over women's mobility, dress and sexuality in the name of protecting "our culture".

::: fig cycle | How the world-system draws on women's labour.
@centre Accumulation in the core
Unpaid household work: subsidises low wages
Subsistence farming: feeds workers cheaply
Export factories: young women's cheap labour
Home-based work: piece rates at the end of supply chains
Migrant care workers: care flows from periphery to core
Remittances: support households left behind
:::

## 6.4  Critical evaluation

::: balance | Does globalisation only increase gender inequality?
- **Evidence of harm:** unsafe factories (the **Rana Plaza** building collapse in Bangladesh, ==24 April 2013==, killed more than 1,100 garment workers, most of them women; the **Ali Enterprises** fire in Karachi, ==2012==); low wages; insecure contracts; care drain; trafficking.
- **Evidence of gains:** export jobs gave millions of young women in Bangladesh, Sri Lanka and elsewhere their **first independent income**, delayed marriage and increased their bargaining power at home. **Naila Kabeer** (*The Power to Choose*, ==2000==) found Bangladeshi garment workers valued their jobs despite poor conditions.
- **Limits of the theory:** world-system theory can make women look like passive victims of a global machine; it underplays **local** patriarchy, **state policy** and women's **agency and organising** (unions, NGOs, fair-trade campaigns).
-> **The verdict:** globalisation is **gendered** — it creates new opportunities and new forms of patriarchal control at the same time. Its effect depends on **labour rights, state policy and women's organisation**.
:::

## 6.5  Pakistan in the world-system

- Pakistan is best placed in the **periphery or lower semi-periphery**: it exports **textiles, cotton and rice**, imports machinery and fuel, and depends on external finance.
- **Women** are concentrated at the bottom of global chains: **home-based workers**, cotton pickers, and workers in garment and football factories.
- **Men's migration** to the Gulf sends home remittances — a record of more than **38 billion dollars** in 2024–25 — while many women manage households alone.
- The EU's **GSP+** trade preferences (from ==2014==) are conditional on Pakistan implementing 27 international conventions, including **CEDAW** and core labour standards — a case of global trade used to push gender and labour rights.

::: eye | The model structure for 2025
1. Introduction: define globalisation and "gendered globalisation"; thesis. 2. World-system theory (Wallerstein 1974; core, semi-periphery, periphery). 3. Its gender blind spot. 4. Feminist extensions: households (Smith and Wallerstein), housewifisation (Mies), global assembly line. 5. New forms of patriarchal power (six). 6. Evidence of harm and of gains (Rana Plaza; Kabeer). 7. Pakistan. 8. Critical evaluation and conclusion. Draw the pyramid or cycle.
:::
